---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { APP_NAME, PUBLIC_REPO_URL } from '../lib/constants'

const reportHref = `${PUBLIC_REPO_URL}/issues/new?title=${encodeURIComponent('Correction or problem')}`
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`How it works | ${APP_NAME}`}
  description="How Scholarship Tracker sources, checks and labels scholarship information for Pakistani students — and how your data stays on your device."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
>
  <article>
    <header class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>How Scholarship Tracker works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A free, independent directory of scholarships for Pakistani students. Every listing is
        built from official pages and documents, with the evidence and the date it was last
        checked.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="what-you-get">
      <h2 id="what-you-get">What every listing shows</h2>
      <ul class="detail-list">
        <li>Who can apply, and the study levels a scholarship accepts</li>
        <li>The documents you need and the steps to apply</li>
        <li>The application timeline and funding on offer</li>
        <li>The official page, a direct apply link when it is verified, and the date we last checked</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        &ldquo;Likely eligible&rdquo; is a conservative guide based on the study levels a
        scholarship accepts. It is not the provider&rsquo;s decision, and it never replaces their
        rules.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="sourcing">
      <h2 id="sourcing">Where the information comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Every listing links to the official sources used &mdash; the provider&rsquo;s own page,
        prospectus or application PDF. We store the evidence quote behind each important fact, so
        you can check it yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        News articles and scholarship aggregators are never treated as facts. They can help us
        discover a programme, but a published detail must be confirmed on the provider&rsquo;s own
        material. A daily automated check revisits each source, records what it finds, and updates
        the last-verified date. When a source cannot be read, the listing is marked provisional
        instead of guessed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="labels">
      <h2 id="labels">What the labels mean</h2>
      <dl class="detail-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>Verified</dt>
          <dd>
            The detail was read on an official page or document, and the supporting quote is kept
            with the listing.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Provisional</dt>
          <dd>
            An official source exists but could not be fully checked &mdash; for example, the page
            blocks automated access, or the deadline appears only in an image or a Facebook post.
            Treat it as a lead, not a fact.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Unverified</dt>
          <dd>
            We could not confirm the detail. An unverified deadline is never shown as a date, and
            an Apply button appears only for a verified, open cycle.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        When something is not confirmed, the listing says so plainly and sends you to the official
        page rather than filling the gap.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="privacy">
      <h2 id="privacy">Your applications stay on your device</h2>
      <p>
        My Applications uses your browser&rsquo;s local storage. There is no account, no email
        address, and no copy on a server. Export a backup code before clearing site data or moving
        to another device, and keep that code private because it contains your notes.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are no advertising trackers. If you use the AI assistant when it becomes available,
        never share personal details &mdash; CNIC or passport numbers, phone numbers, email
        addresses, your address or documents. It does not need them, and it will never ask.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 id="limits">Independence and limits</h2>
      <p>
        {APP_NAME} is independent and not affiliated with any scholarship provider. We do not
        charge for listings and we do not process applications. Scholarship details can change at
        any time, so always confirm eligibility, documents and deadlines on the provider&rsquo;s
        official page before you apply or pay anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is an early public build. The listings currently shown are a small seed set used to
        test the system; coverage and verification will grow as the daily checker runs. If
        something looks wrong or out of date, please tell us.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="report-link" href={reportHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Report an issue or correction
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
